call_details.response:
The board has no immediate 4-in-a-row available for either player among the five legal landings. The only squares that complete a 4 are (2,1) (winning for whoever occupies it), (1,6) (winning only for o) and (0,6) (winning only for x). All three of those squares lie two or more plies below the current landings, so they are not accessible this turn.

Column 1 is therefore poisoned: occupying its lowest empty cell (3,1) immediately hands the opponent the winning cell (2,1). Consequently both players will refuse to play column 1 until every other empty cell has been filled. There are exactly nine such cells. After they are filled it will be x’s turn, forcing x to play (3,1); o then takes (2,1) and wins on the diagonal.

The same nine-cell fill can be completed without ever giving x an unanswerable threat:
- the only threat x can create inside those nine cells is the vertical on column 0 (after x occupies (2,0));
- o simply answers that single threat when it appears;
- the analogous threat on column 6 is likewise a single threat that o answers;
- no move by x can present both threats on the same turn.

Hence every legal column except column 1 preserves o’s forced win. Column 0 is the most direct of those winning moves: it occupies the key cell (2,0) itself, permanently eliminating x’s vertical threat and leaving only the trivial single-threat sequences that o already knows how to answer.

